@@s001 Traits and mixins
@@s002 dato:layer of the object,ora:layer that answers,ok:result
@@s003 <code>new Frog("Kaj") with Registered</code> builds a layered object. At the base the abstract class <b>Animal</b>, with the field name.
@@s004 Frog adds the traits <b>HasLegs</b> (run, jump already written) and <b>Audible</b> (makeNoise only declared).
@@s005 The class <b>Frog</b> writes the method that was missing: makeNoise = "croak!".
@@s006 At the moment of creation <b>Registered</b> is also mixed in: the field id arrives only to this object, not to all frogs.
@@s007 <code>f.makeNoise()</code>: the Frog layer answers.
@@s008 <code>f.run()</code>: Frog does not redefine it, HasLegs answers. And <code>f.id = 42</code> writes into the field of Registered.
@@s009 dato:class,due:interface / trait,ok:inherits code
@@s010 dato:p = (0, 1),due:q = (0, 2),ok:result
@@s011 ora:method that is answering,dato:in the row,ok:result
@@s012 Traits and mixins
@@s013 A <b>trait</b> is a piece of class that you can add to several classes: it can have methods already written, methods to complete, and fields. A class can "mix in" as many as it wants. In exercise 1 of week 3 you use it to add <code>&lt;</code> to a Java class.
@@s014 From Java to Scala
@@s015 In Java a class has <b>only one</b> parent, but it can implement several interfaces. A trait is like a Java interface that can also contain code and fields.
@@s016 Can have…
@@s017 concrete class
@@s018 abstract class
@@s019 trait
@@s020 several parents of the same type
@@s021 no
@@s022 no
@@s023 yes
@@s024 fields (data)
@@s025 yes
@@s026 yes
@@s027 yes
@@s028 methods already written
@@s029 yes
@@s030 yes
@@s031 yes
@@s032 methods to complete (abstract)
@@s033 no
@@s034 yes
@@s035 yes
