@@s039 no
@@s040 Table of slide 8 of week 1. Note: in Scala 3 traits can have parameters, but in the course the rule of the table is used.
@@s041 One parent and several interfaces, or several traits with code
@@s042 The example of the lecture: the frog
@@s043 Classes and traits mixed together
@@s044 An abstract class with a constructor parameter. <code>val</code> in front of the parameter turns it into a field readable from outside: <code>f.name</code>.
@@s045 A trait with two methods <b>already written</b>. Whoever mixes it in receives them ready.
@@s046 A trait with a method <b>without a body</b>: it is abstract. Whoever mixes it in must write it.
@@s047 A trait can have a field. (Here it is a <code>var</code>: it is an example about traits, not functional code.)
@@s048 <code>Frog</code> extends a class and mixes in two traits, separated by commas. The name passed to the constructor goes to <code>Animal</code>.
@@s049 It completes the method that <code>Audible</code> left to write. Without this line <code>Frog</code> does not compile.
@@s050 <b>Mixin at creation</b>: I add a trait to a single object while I create it, even if the class <code>Frog</code> did not foresee it.
@@s051 The object has everything: the field of <code>Registered</code>, the name of <code>Animal</code>, the methods of <code>HasLegs</code>.
@@s052 An object made of layers
@@s053 Exercise 1, week 3: <code>OrderedPoint</code>
@@s054 Goal: to be able to write <code>p &lt; q</code> between two <code>java.awt.Point</code>, a Java class that you cannot modify. The Scala library already has the trait <code>Ordered[T]</code>: if you write the method <code>compare</code>, it gives you <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>.
@@s055 Your solution, line by line
@@s056 My trait extends <code>Ordered[Point]</code>: "I know how to compare myself with a Point". <code>Ordered</code> has only one abstract method, <code>compare</code>.
@@s057 <b>Self-type</b>. It reads: "this trait can be mixed in only inside a <code>java.awt.Point</code>". In exchange I can use <code>this.x</code> and <code>this.y</code>. It is not inheritance: it is a requirement.
@@s058 I write the missing method. Contract: negative number if <code>this</code> is smaller, 0 if equal, positive if larger.
@@s059 <b>Lexicographic</b> order, as in the dictionary: first I compare the <code>x</code>. If they are different, the <code>x</code> decides.
@@s060 Only if the <code>x</code> are equal do I look at the <code>y</code>. If those are equal too, the points are equal: 0.
@@s061 p < q with p = (0, 1) and q = (0, 2)
@@s062 I create the points by mixing the trait in at creation time.
@@s063 <code>&lt;</code> comes from <code>Ordered</code>, which defines it using my <code>compare</code>.
@@s064 The <code>x</code> are equal: I pass to the <code>else</code> branch.
@@s065 The <code>y</code> decides: <code>p</code> is smaller.
@@s066 <code>p &lt; q</code> is true (checked by compiling).
@@s067 The limit of this solution
@@s068 It works only if <b>you create</b> the object yourself, because you must write <code>with OrderedPoint</code>. A Point that reaches you already made by a library does not have the trait. The exercise sheet announces that later the <b>type classes</b> will be seen, which solve exactly this.
@@s069 p < q with Ordered and compare
@@s070 Two traits with the same method: who wins?
@@s071 If two traits redefine the same method, Scala puts them in a row: <b>the one on the right wins</b>, and <code>super</code> passes to the next one in the row.
@@s072 Results checked by compiling. In the course it is enough to know the rule "the rightmost wins".
@@s073 The row of traits: the rightmost wins
